WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:06.062
From the outside, GPU servers still seem to trade over email and forwarded spreadsheets.

2
00:00:06.062 --> 00:00:10.675
The founders say a buyer asking several dealers for similar nodes can get quotes

3
00:00:10.675 --> 00:00:13.988
from two hundred to two hundred forty thousand dollars.

4
00:00:13.988 --> 00:00:18.050
To compare them, someone has to turn each file into a record.

5
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:22.225
And a wrong value that looks clean is worse than one that gets flagged.

6
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.737
Here's the whole system: one pipeline.

7
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:29.375
A file comes in, and its raw bytes and SHA-256 hash are

8
00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:30.563
stored first.

9
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:33.187
A parser reads the spreadsheet or email.

10
00:00:33.187 --> 00:00:36.500
A normalizer maps each value to a strict schema.

11
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.987
Validators check fields against each other.

12
00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:41.875
Every step is an event in an append-only log.

13
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:46.175
And an LLM is optional; it only helps find text in emails.

14
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:51.063
Let's follow one real row: row six of the Helix sample sheet.

15
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:53.600
Ingest hashes the file and logs it.

16
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.937
The spreadsheet parser maps headers through an alias table:

17
00:00:56.937 --> 00:00:59.187
Model becomes GPU SKU.

18
00:00:59.187 --> 00:01:01.950
Warranty has no match, so it's listed as unmapped,

19
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.087
not dropped.

20
00:01:03.087 --> 00:01:07.925
The normalizer turns "H100 SXM" into exactly one catalog SKU,

21
00:01:07.925 --> 00:01:10.363
and "three weeks" into twenty-one days.

22
00:01:10.363 --> 00:01:12.363
Then validate checks the math.

23
00:01:12.725 --> 00:01:16.162
Two hundred thirty-eight thousand, times sixteen nodes,

24
00:01:16.162 --> 00:01:18.813
is three million eight hundred eight thousand.

25
00:01:18.813 --> 00:01:22.013
The sheet says three million eight hundred twenty thousand.

26
00:01:22.013 --> 00:01:25.800
So the total is marked invalid, and the quote goes to review.

27
00:01:26.775 --> 00:01:29.400
Quotes are never stored as rows you can edit.

28
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.588
There are two tables: raw files, and events.

29
00:01:32.588 --> 00:01:36.175
Updates and deletes are blocked twice: by an ORM guard,

30
00:01:36.175 --> 00:01:37.988
and by a database trigger.

31
00:01:37.988 --> 00:01:42.037
To fix a field, a reviewer enters a value and a required reason.

32
00:01:42.037 --> 00:01:44.425
The value goes through the same parser.

33
00:01:44.425 --> 00:01:47.850
The edit becomes a new event, and the quote is validated again.

34
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.800
The original stays in the log.

35
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.275
Emails are messier.

36
00:01:52.275 --> 00:01:54.375
Line-based rules run first.

37
00:01:54.375 --> 00:01:56.850
For fields they miss, a gap-filler steps in:

38
00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:01.338
a deterministic mock by default, or a real LLM if you configure one.

39
00:02:01.338 --> 00:02:05.113
Either way, it only returns a span of text and a line number.

40
00:02:05.113 --> 00:02:08.650
The ground function keeps the span only if it's really on that line,

41
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.137
and the normal parser decides the value.

42
00:02:11.137 --> 00:02:12.962
A made-up span is dropped.

43
00:02:12.962 --> 00:02:14.650
So the model can point at text,

44
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.150
but never mark a field ok by itself.

45
00:02:18.175 --> 00:02:19.638
Now the comparison.

46
00:02:19.638 --> 00:02:21.812
A quote is comparable only if its SKU,

47
00:02:21.812 --> 00:02:24.550
GPUs per node, and condition match the RFQ,

48
00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:26.450
and every field is ok.

49
00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.475
For RFQ-1001, sixteen H100 nodes,

50
00:02:29.475 --> 00:02:31.137
ten quotes qualify.

51
00:02:31.137 --> 00:02:34.138
Thirteen related quotes are excluded, each with a reason:

52
00:02:34.138 --> 00:02:36.650
wrong form factor, four GPUs per node,

53
00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.338
refurbished, euros, expired, or an ambiguous SKU.

54
00:02:40.338 --> 00:02:45.100
To try it, install the requirements and run python -m intake.demo.

55
00:02:46.075 --> 00:02:47.300
A few gotchas.

56
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:50.225
Validity is checked against a pinned dataset date.

57
00:02:50.225 --> 00:02:52.375
Without it, the code uses today's date,

58
00:02:52.375 --> 00:02:54.775
and the samples will expire over time.

59
00:02:54.775 --> 00:02:57.725
The eval shows zero wrong values marked ok,

60
00:02:57.725 --> 00:03:00.425
but the rules and the data generator were written together,

61
00:03:00.425 --> 00:03:02.925
so that says little about real files.

62
00:03:02.925 --> 00:03:06.213
The catalog has seven SKUs and ignores CPU,

63
00:03:06.213 --> 00:03:07.775
network and storage.

64
00:03:07.775 --> 00:03:11.350
Non-dollar prices are always flagged, never converted.

65
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.175
And the quote view is replayed on every request.

66
00:03:15.175 --> 00:03:16.250
To recap.

67
00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.612
Raw bytes and hash are stored first.

68
00:03:18.612 --> 00:03:21.687
Deterministic parsers decide every value.

69
00:03:21.687 --> 00:03:24.238
Anything unclear goes to review with a reason,

70
00:03:24.238 --> 00:03:25.563
never a guess.

71
00:03:25.563 --> 00:03:28.650
Every change is an event, and state is a replay.

72
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.175
And only like-for-like quotes are compared.

73
00:03:32.175 --> 00:03:33.600
Thank you for watching.
